}# LP-01 expectation freeze (Phase A only)

The frozen `repository-wide` Licensing and provenance LP-01 row, clause 1.1,
reads **"Uses an OSI-approved, non-copyleft license."** The requirement basis
specifies an OSI-approved license (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD or equivalent) and
excludes GPL/AGPL for components surfaced from VS. This suite is a
**package classification and optional caller-prevalidated owner/repository
evidence contract**, not an assertion that the owner's entire repository,
other releases, dependencies (LP-03), or notices (LP-04) meet the requirement.
LP-05 establishes only that PackageLicenseExpression
is populated; LP-01 must independently classify a supported expression.
The output is the existing `CaseResult`, never a readiness verdict or score.

## Frozen policy inputs

The checked `corpus` is the unmodified SPDX
[`json/licenses.json`](https://github.com/spdx/license-list-data/blob/d46e94e2c78ceede1cfc63cfa0396472d2798d4c/json/licenses.json)
at tag `v3.27.0`, commit `d46e94e2c78ceede1cfc63cfa0396472d2798d4c`,
SHA-256 `157789ba91984aecba5c67b84168dbbd41a235b910a7569aa64bf42a88b24290`.
Its 699 license IDs and `isOsiApproved` flags are authoritative for
**OSI-list membership only**, not copyleft status or permission to redistribute
a particular package. The independently pinned, reviewable
`policy/lp01-policy-1.0.0.json` classifies a *narrow* set of OSI-approved
identifiers as non-copyleft, strong copyleft or weak copyleft. These
classifications are explicitly **local policy judgments**, not an SPDX
`isCopyleft` field (none exists in this catalog). Policy version, upstream
version and catalog hash must agree, categories must be disjoint, and every
classified ID must be current and OSI-approved. Anything not classified,
even when OSI-approved, escalates to the policy owner; it cannot silently
inherit a permissive classification. Policy and corpus are checked, pinned
inputs in *each* synthetic and retained case, except the missing-policy and
missing-corpus controls. Missing or inconsistent policy fails closed; a changed file digest
fails at the foundation replay loader before row dispatch. The
`no-corpus`, `policy-version-mismatch`, `policy-overlap` and `catalog-version-mismatch`
controls have pinned, intentionally inconsistent bytes; no online lookup is
necessary or authorized to substitute for them.

SPDX IDs are exact and case-sensitive. Only ASCII space, tab, CR and LF
are accepted as expression whitespace; NBSP and em-space are not silently
normalized. `AND` binds more tightly than `OR`, parentheses group, and
every possible `OR` alternative must consist **entirely** of approved,
explicitly non-copyleft licenses for an *eligible package declaration*.
`MIT OR GPL-3.0-only` is an unresolved choice, not a pass; `MIT AND GPL`
is a package-scoped negative; an OR whose *every* alternative contains
strong/weak copyleft is also negative, including `GPL-3.0-only OR MPL-2.0`.
Weak copyleft is not silently treated as non-copyleft. Non-OSI IDs are
negative when every alternative contains one; mixed alternatives escalate.
Syntactically valid `DocumentRef-other:LicenseRef-local` is a custom
reference and escalates, as do `WITH` exceptions, deprecated IDs/`+`
aliases, unknown IDs and unclassified OSI IDs. Reserved operators cannot
appear as exception identifiers (`MIT WITH OR`, `MIT WITH WITH`); these,
invalid operator casing and incomplete/trailing expressions are malformed
and escalate. No normalization turns deprecated identifiers into current
ones or strips an exception. The oracle caps the UTF-8 expression at
**4096 raw UTF-8 bytes and 4096 raw characters BEFORE any trim or normalization**,
**255 tokens**, **16 parenthesis levels** and **128 expanded alternatives**,
counted before deduplication (128 simple OR operands already use 255 tokens).
Composite `(8 alternatives) AND (16 alternatives)` reaches 128 alternatives
in fewer than 255 tokens; the 8-by-17 control exceeds that separate limit.
ASCII trailing-space byte at/over controls and multibyte at/over controls
show that non-ASCII input at the raw byte cap is invalid syntax, while one
raw UTF-8 byte over the cap is `expression-limit`, even though the character
count is within the cap. The precise precedence is **raw UTF-8 bytes/characters first, allowed ASCII
characters second, tokenization/token cap third, then parsing with depth and
alternative caps at the point reached**. Thus an invalid early character
preempts later token overflow, token overflow preempts a trailing syntax error,
and earlier syntax preempts later depth/alternative overflow; a reached depth
or alternative cap preempts trailing syntax. At/over fixtures for all four
bounds test fail-closed `expression-limit`; limits are expectation-contract
choices for Phase B, not an assertion that the current reader already
enforces them. These decisions are bounded to the pinned catalog and local
policy, not legal opinions.

**An OSI-approved, non-copyleft package expression resolves LP-01 for the
checked package declaration.** Every allowlisted expression without an owner
record, including both retained MIT packages, yields an applicable deterministic
finding, mapped to `verified`, with no missing fact, required input or next category.
The requirement does not impose an owner-authorized or release-scoped grant prerequisite.
The absence of an owner record always uses the same
`approved-non-copyleft` branch and evidence contract for eligible declarations,
including the owner-path `no-record` control and both retained packages.
Case IDs, input paths, package identities and unrelated repository metadata
must not select a different no-record result. This affirmative classification
is limited to the exact package declaration; it does not legally interpret
other repository files or other releases or establish owner authority.
Decisive negatives report only that an authoritative *package declaration*
offers no acceptable alternative; they are not repository-wide conclusions.
All other branches, including file-only declarations and supplied owner/grant
validation, retain their existing decisions.

## Prevalidated owner evidence contract (v1)

The required checked roles are `package`, `policy`, and `corpus`. An optional
single `owner-record` and exactly one `grant-<lowercase-license-id>` role
per distinct license ID in the normalized owner expression permit the separate
`owner-evidence-accepted` branch under **the same resolver version**; absence
of the owner record yields `approved-non-copyleft` for an eligible expression.
A secondary `owner-record-conflict` always conflicts rather than
winning by input order. Unknown, extra, duplicate, or missing roles are
rejected or escalated as appropriate **before conversion to a role dictionary**.
The foundation hashes each checked input, with normalized relative paths and
lowercase SHA-256, before dispatch. No absolute path, `..`, `.` segment,
backslash alias or non-normalized separator is accepted in a checked input
or owner grant binding. `rejected-inputs.json` freezes six shared-loader
rejections separately because invalid input paths/duplicate roles cannot be
replayed as successful `cases.json` cases.

The owner record is canonical UTF-8 JSON without BOM, with LF newline and
the **fixed v1 field order below regardless of parsed input order**, no
duplicate/unknown top-level or nested binding keys, and **at most 8192 raw
bytes**. String scalars and prevalidation references are nonempty, at most
256 characters each, NFC, with no NUL/control characters or unpaired
surrogates. Exact JSON types are required; booleans are not integers.
`prevalidation_references` is an ordered array of at most 32 bounded strings.
The 8192/8193-byte records exercise the document cap while every constituent
field remains within its own limit. Its exact v1 fields, in canonical order:

| Field | Required constraint |
|---|---|
| `schema_version` | Integer `1` exactly |
| `caller_accepts_prevalidated_authority` | Boolean `true` for resolution; missing/false escalates |
| `owner_identity`, `owner_reference` | Nonempty, each at most 256 characters; trusted caller-validated identity and reference, not a signature |
| `package_id`, `package_sha256` | Exact checked nuspec ID and checked package digest |
| `repository_url`, `source_commit` | Exact checked nuspec repository URL and full lowercase 40- or 64-hex source commit |
| `release_ref`, `release_version` | Nonempty `refs/tags/` ref accepted from caller, and exact checked nuspec package version. No independent release-ref truth source is supplied |
| `spdx_grant_expression` | Canonical normalized SPDX expression; must be equivalent to the checked package expression |
| `grant_bindings` | Nonempty, sorted array with one binding for **each** distinct license ID in the expression, including all AND and OR terms; no missing, extra or duplicate binding |
| `prevalidation_references` | Bounded caller-supplied references to the prevalidation, with no claim the resolver verifies their authority |
| `policy_version`, `policy_sha256` | Exact checked v1 policy version and digest |
| `spdx_version`, `spdx_sha256` | Exact checked SPDX catalog version and digest |

Every `grant_bindings` element has **exactly**, in field order:
`license_id` (case-sensitive SPDX ID), `role` (the matching checked
`grant-<lowercase-license-id>`), `path` (matching normalized relative checked
input path), `sha256` (matching lowercase digest of independently checked
bytes), and `caller_accepts_complete_grant` (Boolean `true` for resolution).
An `MIT AND Apache-2.0` declaration with only an MIT grant escalates; an OR
also requires grants for **all** offered license IDs. Normalization is
canonical AST normalization: recursively flatten identical AND/OR operators,
sort child nodes deterministically, deduplicate identical nodes, preserve
AND versus OR and WITH/custom-reference semantics. These operations apply
only to expression **equivalence**, after raw parsing and resource checks.
`MIT AND MIT` needs one MIT binding; reordered/flattened equivalent
expressions match, but changed AND/OR grouping does not. No identifier case
folding or deprecated-license substitution is allowed.

The fixture oracle verifies canonical bytes, structural constraints and
cross-input digests, including exact package SHA-256, nuspec package ID,
repository URL, source commit and release version, policy and SPDX versions
and digests, and each grant role/path/digest. For v1, the **caller**
prevalidates owner authority, the release ref's relation to the checked
source/repository/version, grant applicability and legal sufficiency (including
any added restrictions), and explicitly accepts those assertions in the
record and each grant binding. The resolver **does not cryptographically
establish owner authority, tag existence, repository contents or
release-to-source derivation; it does not legally interpret grant text**.
No bespoke vendor signature is invented. A different well-formed release ref
is therefore a caller-accepted assertion, **not** a detectable mismatch without
a second checked scope source. The checked grant bytes must be nonempty,
UTF-8 without BOM and at most **16384 raw bytes**, with **16384 accepted**
and **16385 rejected** in independent checked-grant controls. Grant declaration
lines use the exact case-sensitive `SPDX-License-Identifier:` marker at the
start of a line, allowing only leading ASCII space/tab, ASCII space/tab after
the colon, and trailing ASCII space/tab. Only LF, CRLF and CR split lines.
No comment prefix is recognized; a line containing a differently formatted
or case-variant marker fails closed as ambiguous. **Every** declaration line
is checked against its binding: any contradictory ID fails closed. Removing
all recognized declaration lines and whitespace must leave nonempty grant
text, so no-space, tab-spaced, repeated and indented tags alone cannot count
as a grant, regardless of newline convention. This is a structural screen,
not proof of completeness or a comparison to one MIT fixture. Untagged
caller-prevalidated text and benign copyright variations can qualify; a
grant whose actual restrictions differ from the record remains the caller's
prevalidation responsibility. The local `.gitattributes` preserves grant
fixture bytes without Git newline conversion, including CR-only and CRLF
controls, so their pinned SHA-256 identities are stable in the commit.

`owners/` holds synthetic owner records; `grants/mit-full.txt` and
`grants/bsd-full.txt` are **example grant fixtures**, not vendor evidence.
The 94 owner cases include 14 reachable deterministic positives (including
multi-license AND and OR, equivalent normalized expressions, a bounded
8192-byte record, an exact 16384-byte complete grant, and benign grant
variation). Their findings explicitly
disclose that caller-prevalidated authority was accepted at the input boundary
and was **not cryptographically proven**. Missing one term, contradictory
grant identifiers, non-equivalent ASTs, unaccepted authority/completeness,
tag-only/inconsistent/missing grant bytes, malformed or oversized canonical
records, invalid paths/roles and conflicting records escalate. Before any
owner record is considered, **the same package-reader classification used by
package-only controls** checks ZIP/root nuspec, XML/DTD, namespace and metadata
ambiguity, direct license type/version/attributes, expression grammar and
resource limits, and explicit packaged license declarations. Owner-bound
controls cover duplicate license and metadata, URL/file license types,
unsupported version/attributes, DTD/entity, foreign metadata, multiple root
nuspecs, contradictory packaged LICENSE.txt, malformed/over-limit expression
and GPL. Their non-positive package branches take precedence over even valid
owner evidence: GPL yields a package-scoped copyleft negative, never a
grant-contradiction or positive. These are
frozen expectation branches, **not** production implementation or a statement
that the retained vendors supplied owner evidence.

A URL alone, including `https://licenses.nuget.org/MIT`, is not package
license evidence. A URL beside a recognized expression is not independently
parsed as a license grant, even if its string hints at a different license.
A `type=file` declaration without exact supported in-package evidence
escalates. One synthetic file carries *only*
`SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT`: that tag permits classification of the
identifier but provides **no MIT grant text**, so `file-declared` remains
escalated pending a complete owner-authorized grant and release/source
scope. Full license text interpretation is deliberately **not supported**
by this oracle; a file-only text/filename is no deterministic fallback.
A different synthetic file explicitly declares GPL against
a nuspec MIT expression, so the conflict escalates; filenames and vague
license prose are not a safe conflict detector. No real
retained notice is silently promoted into an SPDX annotation. Shared
nuspec-reader safety and namespace rules take precedence over individual
license facts: malformed XML/DTD, foreign/duplicate metadata, missing or
ambiguous root nuspec and corrupt ZIP escalate. The oracle checks XML using
Expat's doctype/entity declaration callbacks **before** ElementTree can expand
an entity, rather than looking for an ASCII byte sequence. UTF-8 and BOM-marked
UTF-16 LE/BE clean nuspecs remain supported; their used and unused DTD variants
are `unsafe-package`, including when the package has an otherwise eligible
owner record. ZIP names must be unique across **all** entries under Unicode
`casefold()` comparison before any by-name read. This deliberately fails
closed for exact duplicate names, case aliases (`LICENSE.txt`/`license.txt`)
and duplicates of unrelated entries such as `README.txt`; no later entry
can replace a contradictory GPL license with MIT. The duplicate controls
pin entry order and bytes by ZIP entry identity, not by ambiguous filename.
An unavailable package
or a missing/inconsistent policy produces `unknown` applicability.
Unbound/incorrect SHA-256 input paths fail in the shared replay loader;
they are not equivalent to a valid case with no package role.

`cases.json` freezes **198 cases** (104 package and 94 owner) and **197**
reproducible, SHA-256-pinned synthetic archives, alongside six separately
frozen pre-dispatch rejections in `rejected-inputs.json`.
`lp01_expectations.py` checks the exact JSON, ZIP bytes, owner
records and separately checked grant bytes, including
portable entry metadata; `--write-fixtures` only regenerates these
reviewed expectation artifacts. No shipping resolver, registry entry,
runner implemented count or prior LP-05--LP-10/PI-01 fixture changes are
part of this freeze. With this branch's PI-01 tip, coverage remains
**7 implemented / 53 TBD**. The production resolver must later consume
the checked bytes, including pinned policy and catalog, and supply its own
safe bounded ZIP and SPDX parser; the Python oracle is not production code.

## Retained package identities (local opt-in)

| Vendor | SHA-256 | Nuspec SHA-256 | Package policy evidence (LP-01 verified declaration) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Syncfusion `Syncfusion.Blazor.Toolkit` 2.0.1 | `87cf6795afe5106c9d6c4db5e63e7f0ef0c741a850243557b3bbab1854c6121d` | `e9a55efdfc3385c1df49206839689f3f01c0bf5731b6130f05a601632a77acb7` | MIT is OSI-approved and on local non-copyleft allowlist |
| Infragistics `IgniteUI.Blazor.Lite` 0.3.0 | `e9565c7c7093ff3798b62abdc2e67fe9bc438807eb0fa9b9b43f4573f9df0e63` | `8399959e38391faa853f874f6d4c2d10a2724f3e978af5fc79fbd46a7a74833c` | MIT is OSI-approved and on local non-copyleft allowlist |

Both exact nuspecs declare `<license type="expression">MIT</license>` and a
legacy MIT license URL. The archives contain an MIT-headed packaged license
file; the Infragistics archive also has third-party notices, whose
dependency coverage belongs to LP-03. `retained-inputs.json` pins the
archives, nuspec and notice entry identities, without storing proprietary
bytes or absolute workstation paths in Git. Its source hints are not
identities. The component baseline report is not an LP-01 package verdict.
`acceptance.json` freezes **two applicable deterministic findings** with independent
MIT classification in `evidence_contract`, nonempty declaration-scoped findings,
and null `missing_fact`, `required_input` and `next_category`. They map to LP-01
`verified` without owner grant or release-to-repository scope evidence; they
do not establish owner authority or classify other releases. A future policy decision must review all remaining
OSI IDs and exception interactions explicitly rather than loosening
fail-closed handling by inference.

Run from the repository root:

```powershell
python eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp01_expectations.py
python -O eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp01_expectations.py
$env:LP01_SYNCFUSION_NUPKG = 'C:\path\to\syncfusion.blazor.toolkit.2.0.1.nupkg'
$env:LP01_INFRAGISTICS_NUPKG = 'C:\path\to\igniteui.blazor.lite.0.3.0.nupkg'
python eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp01_expectations.py --require-retained
python -O eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp01_expectations.py --require-retained
python eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp01_expectations.py --check-negative-bindings
python -O eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp01_expectations.py --check-negative-bindings
```

Default unbound mode prints `skipped` for each vendor; required mode fails
before archive access unless both bindings are present. To replay retained
cases **after Phase B**, copy `acceptance.json`, the pinned `policy/`
files, and verified vendor packages into the same temporary directory
layout. Never check proprietary archives into this repository.
`acceptance-receipt.template.json` keeps byte verification distinct from
future production replay: its replay fields remain null in Phase A.
# LP-02 public-availability v1.1 contract

## Purpose and stage boundary

`lp02-public-source/1.1.0` establishes a **qualified public-source-availability
fact for the exact checked package**. Only `public-source-observed` is
positive. Its finding states the package-declared repository, exact commit
and capture time, the anonymous retrieval and checked nonempty archive root.
The package declaration supplies the release association; source relevance is
not independently established. The finding explicitly says that this does
**not** establish that the published binaries were built from that revision.
Clause 1.2 requires public source for shipped versions, not a particular SDK,
literal project PackageId, or MSBuild evaluation. Project facts are supplemental.

GitHub codeload can serve fork-network commit objects through the declared
repository URL. Successful retrieval therefore does **not** prove that the
commit belongs to that repository's branches or tags. The positive finding
states this qualification; LP-02 does not perform a network membership check.

Do not infer repository ownership, complete build closure, reproducible
builds, binary/source correspondence, license compliance or overall
readiness. Stronger correspondence/provenance belongs to LP-10/PI-11.
No release tag, private-source conclusion, build reproduction, embedded
source-repository manifest or caller acceptance boolean is part of LP-02.
The copied rubric/basis remain byte-pinned; this is the qualified operational
contract for this handler, not a rewrite of those source documents.

Acquisition may retrieve source; the deterministic resolver is **offline**.
It only consumes `CheckedInput` bytes already SHA-256-validated by dispatch.
It does not reopen paths, inspect Git state, read credentials, execute code,
invoke `gh`, evaluate MSBuild or access the network.

## Supported package and source profile

- Ordinary, bounded NuGet ZIPs: at most 64 MiB compressed and 64 MiB total
  uncompressed, 4096 entries, 8 MiB central directory, and safe ASCII relative
  entry paths of at most 256 characters. Source archives additionally admit
  interior ASCII spaces in path segments, reusing the existing safe inspection
  predicate; leading/trailing spaces remain unsupported. Package paths retain
  the stricter profile. Reject dot/dot-dot segments,
  backslashes, absolute paths, case aliases, symlinks, encryption, Zip64,
  unsupported flags/methods, inconsistent headers/lengths/CRCs and unsupported
  archive framing. ZIP storage and normal Deflate use the existing bounded
  archive validator, not a new codec or decompressor. The standard root
  NuGet packaging entry `[Content_Types].xml` is explicitly supported.
- Exactly one root nuspec, at most 1 MiB. Reuse the recognized NuGet namespace
  and metadata-sibling rules. Accept strict UTF-8 (optional BOM) or BOM-marked
  UTF-16 LE/BE; if present, the XML encoding declaration must say `utf-8`
  or `utf-16` respectively, case-insensitively. DTDs/entities are prohibited.
  Exactly one nonblank, simple direct ASCII id/version (at most 256 characters)
  and no ambiguous repository
  element may supply identity. Repository attributes are limited to unqualified
  `type`, `url`, `commit`, `branch`; URL/commit must both be nonblank.
- Repository profile: `https://github.com/{owner}/{repo}`, optionally normalizing
  a terminal `.git`. No userinfo, query, fragment, port, trailing slash,
  arbitrary host, path encoding or ref/tag lookup. Owner/repository use the
  documented implementation's bounded ASCII GitHub-name pattern. Commit is
  a full nonzero 40-hex SHA-1, normalized to lowercase. Other object formats
  and environments are unresolved, not evidence of closed source.
- Retain the complete bounded codeload ZIP. All files must be under the
  `{repo}-{commit}/` root (case-insensitive root comparison), and at least one
  file must exist. Empty and directory-only archives reject. Optional selected
  supplemental files have exact archive paths and SHA-256 digests, but project
  or implementation selection is not required. Source ZIPs may have
  GitHub's 40-byte exact-commit archive comment (or no comment); arbitrary
  comments are unsupported. Package ZIPs remain comment-free, preserving
  LP-01's existing validator behavior.
- Razor or other SDKs, conditional declarations, imports, targets and inherited
  build files do not gate public availability. No project XML or implementation
  bytes are evaluated for relevance. Selected supplemental files, if supplied,
  must still exist and match their exact digests within archive limits.
  The schema retains a nonblank ASCII `mapping_rationale` of at most 1000
  characters for compatibility; it describes the declaration/acquisition
  association, not an independently proven library-source mapping.

Outside-profile archives or acquisition evidence escalate conservatively.
Project language, package-ID inference and arbitrary build evaluation are not
part of this availability check.

## Acquisition procedure

The installed eleven-row helper produces an observation from its own single
successful anonymous codeload download, using the original request timestamp,
exact package ID spelling, and retained archive digest. It supplies an empty
supplemental inventory. Local imports, offline mode, unsuccessful downloads and
budget skips never generate public-retrieval observations. Explicitly supplied
checked observation/archive packets remain replayable offline.

The separate repository-only acquisition command remains compatible. If using it,
supply exact supplemental paths, including the commit root, and a short rationale:

```powershell
dotnet run --project eng\blazor-package-resolver\BlazorPackageResolver.csproj -- acquire-lp02 package.nupkg capture-new repo-COMMIT/src/Library/Library.csproj repo-COMMIT/src/Library/Widget.cs "The literal PackageId identifies this library; Widget.cs is adjacent library implementation."
```

The command reads and validates the package before constructing the one fixed
URL `https://codeload.github.com/{owner}/{repo}/zip/{commit}`. Its dedicated
HTTP handler disables default/explicit credentials, cookies, proxy discovery,
redirects and automatic decompression; it supplies only a fixed user agent.
It does not use browser state, `gh`, Git helpers, API tokens or ambient
application authentication. Normal TLS certificate validation and revocation
checks remain enabled. Environments requiring proxies or credentials are
unsupported; do not weaken the transport to obtain a positive.

One GET, no retries or fallback hosts, 60-second total timeout, 16 KiB
response-header setting and 64 MiB streamed response cap bound retrieval.
Only HTTP 200 without Content-Encoding retains `source.zip`.
404 -> `not-found`; 401/403 -> `auth-required`; 429/451 -> `blocked`;
5xx/network/timeout -> `unavailable`; redirects, other statuses, encoded or
oversized responses -> `unsupported`. Every such result generates
`observation.json`, with no success capture/inventory for unresolved attempts.
The destination must not already exist. Filesystem failures are CLI errors,
not successful captures. Preserve the generated receipt and archive together;
do not hand-author an access assertion.

The command hashes source files at the explicitly supplied paths; it does not
force them to be relevant. Missing explicit selections are acquisition errors,
not silently omitted inventory entries. The helper does not invoke this command
after downloading: that would perform a second GET outside the collector's budget.

## Observation schema and checked bindings

`observation.json` is strict UTF-8 JSON, maximum 16 KiB and depth 8, with
duplicate/unknown fields rejected. There are no acceptance booleans.
Malformed UTF-8, escaped Unicode or deferred JSON string/field decoding
failures produce `observation-invalid`, never an unhandled exception.

```json
{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "producer": "lp02-anonymous-github/1.0.0",
  "package_id": "Sample.Library",
  "package_version": "1.0.0",
  "package_sha256": "<64 lowercase hex>",
  "repository_url": "https://github.com/owner/repo",
  "commit": "<40 lowercase hex>",
  "retrieval_attempt": {
    "url": "https://codeload.github.com/owner/repo/zip/COMMIT",
    "observed_at_utc": "2026-10-30T12:00:00Z",
    "transport": "direct-https-no-credentials/1",
    "result": "retrieved",
    "http_status": 200
  },
  "capture_receipt": {
    "role": "source-archive",
    "sha256": "<64 lowercase hex>"
  },
  "source_inventory": [],
  "mapping_rationale": "The package declares this repository and revision; anonymous retrieval retained this archive."
}
```

Use checked roles `package`, `source-observation`, `source-archive` in the
existing schema-version-1 replay suite; pin each file's path and SHA-256.
The receipt binds exact package ID/version/digest, normalized repository,
commit and codeload URL; the capture digest binds the retained archive;
the optional inventory binds up to two unique selected paths/kinds
(`project`, `implementation`) and their actual bytes without semantic mapping.
An unresolved attempt instead has `capture_receipt: null` and an empty
inventory; the status must match its result. A transport failure has
`http_status: null`. UTC times must use `yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ`.

These are machine-generated acquisition observations at a checked-evidence
boundary, **not signed attestations**. Offline hash checks cannot prove that
a dishonest party did not fabricate a receipt/archive or independently attest
the transport's history. Use the supplied acquisition operation and preserve
its output; never substitute a hand-authored `public=true`, correspondence
assertion or a repository-embedded `.lp02-source.json`.

## Ordered branches and status

| Branch | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `missing-package` | Exact package absent |
| `package-unreadable` | Package/identity outside the safe unambiguous profile |
| `source-declaration-missing` | Package has no complete URL/commit declaration |
| `public-retrieval-unresolved` | Declaration outside retrieval profile, or retained attempt unresolved |
| `observation-missing` | Required checked observation or successful attempt's archive absent |
| `observation-invalid` | Unsupported/malformed receipt, inventory or source archive |
| `observation-binding-conflict` | Exact package/revision/archive/selected-byte bindings disagree |
| `source-relevance-unestablished` | A supplied supplemental selected file is missing (legacy branch name) |
| `public-source-observed` | The sole qualified positive |

Validation order follows package -> declaration/profile -> observation shape
-> package/retrieval binding -> attempt state -> archive binding/profile ->
optional selected-file bindings. Every nonpositive result is an
escalation with unknown applicability and no factual finding: unresolved /
`not tested`, **not** closed-source or a gap.

## Focused validation and retained replay

There are **51 named synthetic cases**: 33 oracle/replay cases and
18 runtime cases covering generated anonymous observations, unresolved HTTP
states, network failure, header/stream caps, receipt/archive resource
boundaries, path/root safety, optional inventory and missing explicit selections,
checked-buffer/culture determinism, non-gating Razor/MSBuild/encoding variants,
malformed receipt Unicode, empty/directory-only archive rejection, and interior
space admission with traversal/edge-space/platform-path rejection.
Runtime cases group related boundary/status assertions;
they are not an exhaustive cross-runtime differential matrix. The independent
Python oracle specifies expected branches and bytes without importing or
invoking production code. The ordinary tests never access the network.

```powershell
python eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp02_expectations.py
dotnet run --project eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\ResolverChecks.csproj -- lp02
python eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\lp01_expectations.py
dotnet run --project eng\blazor-package-resolver\tests\ResolverChecks.csproj
dotnet run --project eng\blazor-package-resolver\BlazorPackageResolver.csproj -- coverage
```

`--write` on the Python oracle materializes its specified synthetic inputs
and expectations, never production results. `acceptance.json` separately
pins the two vendor packages using the already accepted LP-01 retained
identities. Supply both `LP02_SYNCFUSION_NUPKG` and
`LP02_INFRAGISTICS_NUPKG` to run actual retained replay. The runner verifies
their exact byte counts/digests, stages only those supplied bytes, and replays
the frozen expectations. The binaries remain outside Git. Both expected
outcomes are `observation-missing`, qualified **declared-source-unverified**,
until real checked anonymous acquisition evidence and the bound archive are supplied.
Do not infer a positive from their repository declarations.
# LP-03 dependency-license expectation contract (Phase A)

**Frozen row:** LP-03; repository-wide; Licensing and provenance; clause 1.3.
**Direct obligation:** "All direct and transitive dependencies use OSI-approved,
non-copyleft licenses."

This is the Phase A contract for the proposed
`lp03-dependency-licenses/1.0.0`, not a production resolver, acquisition
implementation, registry change, legal approval or overall readiness verdict.
LP-03 remains `tbd`. The accepted base is LP-02 correction commit
`3db9cf49730748bb70a43311928fc99d4ef040d7`.

## Readiness boundary and supported profile

`verified` requires a complete, exact-version direct/transitive inventory for
**every explicitly assessed target**, exact root and dependency bytes, and
applicable retained license evidence for **every distinct dependency version**.
An inventory, a filename, an SPDX tag, PackageLicenseExpression, an SPDX URL,
or a package-manager success exit code alone is insufficient. A demonstrated
incompatible license supports `gap`. Missing or ambiguous evidence is
unresolved; its absence is not a license classification.

The smallest supported evidence combination is:

1. The checked root nupkg and an independent `assessment` selecting its exact
   ID, version, SHA-256 and nonempty set of target frameworks.
2. A checked **full resolved target graph export**, plus a checked machine-generated
   acquisition receipt binding its bytes, root, exact target set, per-target
   node/edge counts and complete distinct-version package inventory.
3. Every inventoried dependency nupkg, its observed identity and single license
   declaration, its separately retained packaged grant, and a checked,
   **caller-prevalidated applicability review** binding that dependency digest,
   grant digest and license ID to the entire dependency version.
4. The exact accepted LP-01 policy 1.0.0 and SPDX 3.27.0 catalog bytes.

Two trust boundaries are explicit, not disguised as cryptographic proof:

- **Acquisition/closure:** the accepted bounded producer must export the entire
  already-resolved package graph for all requested targets, including direct,
  transitive, build/analyzer and assetless package nodes, without filtering
  down to runtime assemblies. It must observe package identities, license
  declarations and actual packaged grant entries from the checked archives.
  The graph is the complete resolved export, not a caller's selected list.
  Receipt counts, reachability and hashes detect inconsistency, not deliberate
  fabrication or a producer that consistently omits a dependency. Producer
  strings and capture references are not signatures or authority checks.
- **Applicability review:** before admitting a review, the caller validates the
  reviewer's authority, the grant's completeness, and its applicability to the
  **entire exact dependency version**, including bundled material. A packaged
  LICENSE file or metadata declaration cannot establish those facts by itself.
  Review references identify that prevalidation, not network lookups. The
  resolver does not infer a grant from its prose or prove reviewer authority.
  This deliberately reuses LP-01's disclosed prevalidation boundary, not a
  new automatic license scanner or a claim that vendors supplied reviews.

The two accepted boundaries are conditions of a qualified positive. Arbitrary
caller-written captures/reviews are not admissible acquisition or legal
evidence merely because they satisfy the JSON schema. The caller must enforce
admission; the offline classifier cannot authenticate either boundary.
Synthetic records below stipulate these conditions **only for fictional
packages**. They are never evidence about the retained vendors.

The supported graph is `resolved-target-graph/1`, a language-neutral JSON
export of a previously resolved graph. It is **not** `project.assets.json`,
packages.lock.json, a project, a version-range solver, a NuGet dependency
selection algorithm, an SBOM completeness heuristic or a restore emulator.
No arbitrary source format is accepted by renaming it to a graph. A future
bounded acquisition adapter must obtain and preserve this full export and
issue the receipt; Phase A does not implement such an adapter. No `complete`,
`accepted` or `success` boolean substitutes for this evidence.

Acquisition may use package tooling in its own stage. The future resolver
uses only immutable, SHA-256-checked buffers: **no network, restore,
credentials, package-manager execution, environment-selected policy, Git
inspection, code execution or reopening paths**. Existing bounded package
inspection can be reused; do not rebuild ZIP/XML runtimes. Outside-profile
packages and unsupported capture formats escalate, never silently fall back.

## Checked roles, schemas and exact bindings

`cases.json` and `acceptance.json` use the foundation's schema-version-1
replay and `CaseResult` shape. Phase A does **not** run these through the
unimplemented production LP-03 dispatcher. A future replay must compare the
frozen expected results byte-for-byte, not regenerate them from production.

All JSON objects have **exactly** the fields shown below; duplicate and unknown
fields are invalid at every level. Field order is insignificant when reading;
the generator freezes one stable order and ASCII-escaped UTF-8/LF output.
Only integer schema version `1` is supported; booleans are not integers.

| Checked role | Content |
|---|---|
| `package` | Exact assessed root nupkg |
| `assessment` | `{schema_version, root, target_frameworks}` |
| `dependency-graph` | `{schema_version, root, targets, unresolved}` |
| `acquisition-receipt` | `{schema_version, producer, root, graph_sha256, target_frameworks, result, completeness_basis, packages, capture_reference}` |
| `license-reviews` | `{schema_version, reviews}`; absent reviews never create a license conclusion |
| `dependency-NNN` | One nupkg per distinct dependency ID/version |
| `grant-NNN` | Separately retained packaged grant bytes for the corresponding dependency |
| `policy`, `corpus` | Exact byte-pinned LP-01 policy and SPDX catalog |

`root` and every package `identity` are `{id, version, sha256}`. IDs are
canonical lowercase ASCII `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9.-]{0,63}`. Versions are **exact**
three-component decimal strings, each component `0` or 1-5 digits without
leading zero. v1 does not support ranges, floating versions, prereleases,
build metadata, version normalization or inferring the selected version.
TFMs are opaque exact lowercase ASCII `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9.-]{0,63}` names, not
framework compatibility rules. SHA-256 is exactly 64 lowercase hex characters.
Root ID/version must match the inspected root archive, not just another
caller-supplied field. Every dependency identity/declaration and packaged
grant path/digest must match inspection of that exact archive.
Only ASCII ID lowercasing is used when capturing a nuspec identity; license
IDs remain case-sensitive and no general Unicode or version normalization
is implied.

`target_frameworks` in the assessment is authoritative for **assessment scope**.
It must not be inferred from whichever targets were successfully acquired.
The graph, receipt target set and completeness-basis target set must match it
exactly, without duplicates or silently ignored extra targets. A finding
applies only to this explicit target set, not unassessed TFMs or arbitrary
application consumer dependency overrides. RIDs/conditional consumer graphs
are outside v1; they require a new explicit scope/profile, not silent folding.

Each graph target is:

```json
{
  "tfm": "net8.0",
  "direct": ["sample.a/1.0.0"],
  "nodes": [
    {"key": "sample.a/1.0.0", "dependencies": ["sample.b/1.0.0"]},
    {"key": "sample.b/1.0.0", "dependencies": []}
  ]
}
```

Keys are canonical `id/version`, with no unresolved version expressions.
Every direct and transitive edge must name an existing exact node. Every node
must be reachable from `direct`; no orphan inventory, duplicate nodes,
duplicate direct edges or duplicate adjacency edges are allowed. Cycles are
traversed with a visited set, not treated as package-manager resolution.
Within **one target**, two versions of the same ID are unresolved conflicts.
Across different targets, different versions are permitted and **both** must
be inventoried and evidenced. The distinct-version union is sorted by ordinal
ASCII key; its zero-based position assigns `dependency-000`/`grant-000`, etc.
Repeated use of an exact version across TFMs uses one evidence set while
retaining every target's edges. One ID/version cannot name competing digests.
No "highest version wins" or first-target wins rule exists.

`unresolved` is a bounded array of capture diagnostics. It must be empty for
a conclusive result. A zero-dependency finding requires a captured, empty
direct/node graph **for every assessed target** with matching zero counts;
absence of a graph is not a vacuous positive.

The receipt producer is exactly `lp03-resolved-target-capture/1.0.0`.
`result` is `captured` or `unavailable`; unrecognized states are unsupported.
`graph_sha256` binds the entire checked graph. `completeness_basis` is:

```json
{
  "profile": "resolved-target-graph/1",
  "targets": [{"tfm": "net8.0", "node_count": 2, "edge_count": 2}]
}
```

`edge_count` includes root direct edges plus every node's outgoing edges.
Counts alone are insufficient: they must match the retained full graph,
target membership, edge closure, reachability and full package union.
For `unavailable`, the producer emits null graph digest, empty package
inventory and empty basis targets; it cannot represent success. Checked
partial exports may be retained for investigation but remain unresolved.

Each receipt `packages` element is:

```json
{
  "key": "sample.a/1.0.0",
  "identity": {"id": "sample.a", "version": "1.0.0", "sha256": "<64 lowercase hex>"},
  "role": "dependency-000",
  "declared_expression": "MIT",
  "grant": {
    "role": "grant-000",
    "path": "grants/retained.txt",
    "sha256": "<64 lowercase hex>",
    "package_entry": "LICENSE.txt"
  }
}
```

The inventory must equal the graph union, not a superset or subset.
`declared_expression` and `grant` may be null when acquisition lacks that
evidence; that never establishes compatible or incompatible licensing.
`grant.path` and digest bind the separately checked bytes;
`package_entry` and digest bind their actual source within the dependency
archive. v1 selects one inspected packaged grant entry per dependency version;
its safe canonical path is matched **exactly, case-sensitively**, not inferred
from a conventional filename. `LICENSE.txt` above is an example, not a required
name: an actual `licenses/COPYING.txt` entry is equally admissible when its
path, bytes, digest and whole-version applicability review all bind.
No directory scanning, filename guessing or merging multiple grant files is
promised. v1 supports packaged grants only. An external URL, partial excerpt,
tag-only file or caller-named arbitrary text is not a complete grant.
Other evidence sources need a separately reviewed profile.

Each applicability review is:

```json
{
  "key": "sample.a/1.0.0",
  "package_sha256": "<exact dependency digest>",
  "grant_sha256": "<exact retained grant digest>",
  "license_id": "MIT",
  "scope": "entire-dependency-version",
  "basis": "published-package-grant",
  "disposition": "applicable-single-license",
  "reviewer_reference": "reviewer authority prevalidation reference",
  "authority_reference": "grant authority and completeness prevalidation reference",
  "review_reference": "exact-version whole-package applicability prevalidation reference"
}
```

References are mandatory nonblank bounded ASCII strings, not acceptance
booleans. Missing reviews escalate. Duplicate reviews or reviews for a version
outside the inventory conflict; review ordering does not select a winner.
The observed declaration, reviewed license ID and retained grant must agree.
A reviewer cannot override a contradictory declaration to force a positive.
For conclusive classification, disposition is `applicable-single-license`,
basis is `published-package-grant` and the review accepts a complete grant
for the entire dependency version. The classifier only screens supported
structure and checked bindings; grant **sufficiency is caller-prevalidated**.
Fictional negative grants explicitly offer a sole incompatible license by
reference; they do not copy the referenced GPL/LGPL/CC license texts.
Fictional MIT/BSD positives retain complete example grant texts reused from LP-01.

`disposition: unresolved` expresses incomplete applicability research and
routes to `not tested`. The sole owner-only route requires all of:
`license_id`/declaration `LicenseRef-owner-grant`,
`basis: unpublished-owner-grant`, `disposition: owner-grant-required`,
retained packaged evidence that an individual unpublished grant is necessary,
and prevalidated authority/review references establishing that necessity.
The retained file here is evidence of the missing private grant, **not** the
grant itself. Ordinary unavailable public bytes, unknown terms, ambiguity or
failed acquisition must not be mislabeled as requiring an owner.

## Policy, ambiguity and limits

Reuse accepted `lp01-policy-1.0.0.json`, SHA-256
`b395256c51e7c0ae68bf3a213aab90bee0479f6115c3507e456269d0cdd41f47`,
and SPDX license-list 3.27.0, SHA-256
`157789ba91984aecba5c67b84168dbbd41a235b910a7569aa64bf42a88b24290`.
The local copies are byte-identical to LP-01; no live catalog lookup or
runtime policy replacement is authorized. SPDX supplies OSI membership,
not copyleft classification; the finite LP-01 allowlist and strong/weak
copyleft lists are disclosed local policy judgments. No new legal policy
is inferred here. Unclassified OSI IDs, deprecated IDs, unknown IDs and
custom terms are unresolved, not automatically non-OSI gaps.

Only a **single exact current SPDX license ID** is conclusive in v1.
OR/dual choices, AND/conjunctive terms, WITH exceptions, aliases, custom
references and arbitrary license prose all escalate conservatively.
There is deliberately no expression AST or legal interpretation engine.
The one owner-only sentinel above is never a license classification.
Current SPDX IDs explicitly not OSI-approved, or IDs on the accepted
strong/weak copyleft lists, are incompatible **only with applicable retained
evidence**. A conflict between declarations/reviews is unresolved binding
conflict, not a demonstrated incompatible license.

Numerical profile (inclusive bounds):

| Surface | Bound |
|---|---|
| Checked root or dependency archive | 8 MiB each; accepted bounded foundation inspection, no extraction to disk |
| Aggregate checked bytes per case | 64 MiB |
| Each normalized JSON document | 65536 bytes, depth 8, UTF-8 without BOM |
| Targets | 1-4 requested, at most 4 graph/basis targets |
| Distinct dependency versions | 64 across the union and per-target node list |
| Root direct or single-node outgoing edges | 64 |
| All edges per target, including root direct edges | 128 |
| Capture diagnostics/reviews/inventory entries | 64 each |
| Retained grant | 1-16384 bytes, nonblank strict UTF-8 without BOM |
| Ordinary reference, expression or field name | 1-256 printable ASCII characters |
| Checked or packaged grant path | 1-256 characters; canonical safe ASCII relative path |

JSON string decoding has an additional 1024-character ceiling before field
validation. Target/ID limits are 64, package-key limit 128, checked-role limit
64. No booleans, floating-point/nonfinite numbers, controls, non-ASCII JSON
strings or deferred Unicode decoding are supported. Paths use normalized
`/` inside manifests (the foundation convention), never OS-specific aliases,
absolute paths, empty/dot/dot-dot segments or backslashes. Nothing is executed
or extracted. Existing archive safety applies; this contract adds no custom
ZIP codecs, XML evaluation, license-text parser or case-folding machinery.
Oversized/unsupported evidence is `not tested`, not a licensing gap.

## Ordered branches, results and readiness interpretation

The result remains the existing non-scoring `CaseResult`: exact inputs,
`input:role:sha256` references, contract, finding **or** missing fact/input/
next category. `verified`, `gap`, `not tested` and `owner evidence required`
are the **frozen interpretation in `acceptance-manifest.json`**, not new
production result properties or organizational approval.

| Branch | Interpretation | Decision / applicability |
|---|---|---|
| `missing-package` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `scope-missing` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `evidence-missing` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `input-invalid` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `policy-unavailable` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `binding-conflict` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `closure-unresolved` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `dependency-bytes-missing` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `license-evidence-unresolved` | not tested | escalated / unknown |
| `owner-evidence-required` | owner evidence required | escalated / applicable |
| `incompatible-dependency-license` | gap | deterministic / applicable |
| `dependencies-policy-satisfied` | verified (qualified) | deterministic / applicable |

Pre-dispatch loader failures are **rejections**, never row results: duplicate
roles, unsafe paths, absent pinned files, changed pinned bytes or unknown
input-binding fields. A deliberately omitted role is distinguishable from a
manifest claiming a file that cannot be read. Optional known roles absent
from the manifest may escalate; a bad claimed digest always rejects first.

Ordered evaluation:

1. Shared pinned-input validation; then missing root, then missing assessment.
2. Parse/shape/resource checks for all supplied normalized documents and roles.
3. Exact policy/catalog pins.
4. Inspected root identity/digest versus scope; receipt existence and root binding.
5. Acquisition state; graph existence and exact root/graph digest bindings.
6. Exact target sets, resolved edges, same-target version uniqueness,
   reachability, completeness counts and inventory equality.
7. All dependency identity/declaration/role and actual packaged grant bindings,
   and all review bindings; extra evidence roles conflict.
8. Missing dependency bytes; incomplete/unsupported license evidence; genuinely
   owner-only evidence; demonstrated incompatible license; otherwise positive.

At steps 7-8, validate **all** binding conflicts before choosing a missing
package or license outcome; sorted node traversal cannot hide a later bad
binding. This deliberately conservative profile requires a coherent complete
evidence packet before reporting a gap: an incomplete graph, unavailable
dependency, missing other grant or ambiguous other license takes precedence
over an otherwise evidenced incompatible node. It may defer a real
counterexample for repair, but cannot create a gap from missing information.
All review/binding conflicts remain unresolved rather than selecting whichever
license appears first. No negative finding is emitted on escalations.

## Independent oracle, fixtures and retained roots

There are **50 named expectations total**: 43 synthetic replay cases, 5
pre-dispatch loader rejections and 2 retained-root expectations. Each replay
case's rationale states the normative rule; there is no cross-runtime ZIP,
XML, package-manager or expression-parser differential matrix.
The retained roots cover the `scope-missing` branch; the other 11 branches
have synthetic replay witnesses. The unique-ID and total-count checks include
all three groups, not just replay cases.

`lp03_expectations.py` constructs known deterministic synthetic nupkgs with
literal exact dependency groups, graph exports, receipts, retained grants and
reviews. It records identity/declaration/grant facts **at byte construction**
and uses those facts for oracle comparisons, rather than implementing a
second archive/XML runtime. It is **not a general nupkg classifier** and
cannot classify arbitrary vendor archives. Phase B must use the existing
bounded archive/nuspec reader to check these same inspected-byte facts.
The independently specified branch table is compared with the receipt oracle
and complete expected `CaseResult` objects; no production code is imported or
invoked. Checks use explicit exceptions, not Python `assert`, so optimized
execution retains the same safeguards.

### Discriminating correction to the unaccepted matrix

The initial Phase A matrix at
`b91de23a90490f6f219e3183d5c19d160da498ea` was not accepted: several
bad packets failed earlier redundant checks, and important omitted-evidence
and second-target decisions were not distinguishable. The first correction,
`489e75508834c740aaf191c85dea39541b685f2b`, replaced **17** replay IDs
with **20** new IDs, retained **23**, and changed
no policy classifications, readiness interpretations or production behavior.
The prospective resolver version stays 1.0.0; this is a correction before
Phase A acceptance, not a released resolver upgrade.

The following IDs are retired (all use the `lp03-` prefix):

```text
missing-scope, single-mit-at-grant-limit, direct-transitive,
multi-tfm-shared, direct-gpl, metadata-only, owner-grant,
wrong-root-digest, missing-tfm, unreachable-node, wrong-grant-binding,
wrong-package-binding, wrong-graph-binding, wrong-review-binding,
missing-policy, incomplete-before-gap, binding-before-gap
```

The replacement/addition IDs and the isolated obligations are:

| Case ID suffix | Distinguishing rule |
|---|---|
| `missing-reviews`, `missing-one-review` | Entire review document, or exactly one review, absent with all grants/packages intact |
| `multi-tfm-second-gap`, `multi-tfm-second-unresolved` | Only a dependency unique to target two supplies a gap or missing grant |
| `osi-unclassified` | Current OSI-approved EUPL-1.2 remains outside every finite local classification |
| `deprecated-id` | Deprecated BSD-2-Clause-FreeBSD is unresolved before its non-OSI catalog flag could imply a gap |
| `with-expression` | Do not strip an exception to obtain MIT |
| `archive-declaration-mismatch` | Receipt/review agree with each other, not the actual archive declaration |
| `review-unresolved`, `review-nonaccepting-basis` | Each review acceptance field is independently necessary |
| `review-package-binding` | Only the review's package digest is wrong |
| `packaged-grant-binding` | Retained bytes and all outer digests agree, but not the packaged grant entry |
| `receipt-target-mismatch` | Only the receipt target set differs from scope/graph/basis |
| `wrong-graph-root` | The graph is rehashed correctly but targets different root bytes |
| `dependency-role-index` | Both receipt and checked dependency role are consistently renamed to the wrong index |
| `duplicate-reviews`, `out-of-inventory-review` | Neither identical duplicate nor extra unassessed review may be ignored |
| `unexpected-evidence-role` | An extra valid checked grant role conflicts with the inventory |
| `owner-before-gap` | A genuine owner-only requirement wins over another fully evidenced incompatible node |
| `later-binding-before-unresolved` | First-node missing grant cannot hide a later-node review/package binding conflict |

Retained cases are tightened rather than duplicated: `multi-tfm-versions`
also covers shared transitive-version deduplication, direct/transitive evidence
and the inclusive 16384-byte grant bound. `same-tfm-version-conflict` now has
every package, review and count present, so missing inventory cannot mask
version ambiguity. `incomplete-basis` has otherwise complete GPL evidence
to protect closure-before-gap precedence. `dangling-edge` retains all
existing nodes' reachability, and `unknown-field` uses a valid string value
so an unrelated unsupported JSON scalar cannot mask the unknown-field check.
Missing assessment scope is still exercised against both actual retained
roots when their bindings are supplied. Changed-byte loader controls and
actual retained bindings retain the root-byte pin checks.

Every changed receipt, graph, review and retained input is rehashed. In
particular, the packaged-grant mismatch updates the retained path, checked
digest, receipt digest and review digest together while leaving the actual
dependency archive intact; only the claimed packaged-entry binding fails.

### Final bounded guard-inventory correction

The correction on top of `489e75508834c740aaf191c85dea39541b685f2b`
keeps **exactly the same 43 replay, 5 loader and 2 retained expectations**.
`cases.json`, all 214 checked inputs (576483 bytes), their input manifest,
the loader controls and retained expectations are byte-identical.
No retired replay ID is reintroduced. The only classification-profile change
removes the arbitrary literal `LICENSE.txt` restriction in favor of the actual
inspected source path and digest. Root/dependency archive facts are looked up
by their checked byte digests; digest comparisons and inspected ID/version
comparisons are independent, with unchanged normal classification semantics.

The two-guard follow-up to `8626b46ee47780096750e21476bc4269d86f36b8`
adds only `empty-target-scope` and `capture-producer`, preserving all prior
64 guard registrations and 75 witnesses without changing classification
semantics or any named expectation/input bytes. The first recipe copies the
existing zero-dependencies positive and consistently empties assessment,
graph, receipt and count-target lists. The second copies a complete positive
and changes only the receipt producer to an unaccepted value. Every affected
digest is recomputed. Both normally return `input-invalid`; weakening only
the respective nonempty-scope or accepted-producer guard must instead yield
the wrong `dependencies-policy-satisfied` branch.

`discrimination.json` now freezes **66 acceptance-critical guard IDs** and
**77 registered witnesses**:

- The original **24 weakened variants / 28 existing-case executions** are
  retained unchanged.
- **33 explicit in-memory recipes** copy existing complete packets; together
  with the original probes, **49 distinct weakened variants / 61 executions**
  must reach their prescribed wrong branch.
- **16 existing-case branch witnesses** register remaining already-covered
  branch obligations and an explicit rejected counterexample branch. These
  recheck existing expected results; they are **not** additional weakened
  implementations or newly named replay expectations.

For each weakened probe, the ordinary oracle on that exact packet must first
reach its explicit expected branch; weakening the named guard must then reach
the different, explicit **wrong branch**. Throwing an exception, or falling
through to another `binding-conflict`, does not count. All unrelated outer
checked-input digests and changed graph/receipt/review bindings are recomputed
and passed through the checked-input loader. These copies and any newly
constructed archive observations are used in memory only, then discarded.
The checked fixture files and the normal evidence policy are not changed.

The recipes isolate checked grant path and digest, review grant digest,
actual packaged entry path, graph digest, graph target membership independently
of receipt membership, reachability, missing policy and missing catalog,
dependency digest/ID/version/key, grant-role index, inventory subset/superset/
duplicates, and each owner sentinel prerequisite. The graph-target recipe
changes only target two in the graph and its counts, leaving scope and receipt
targets intact. The unreachable-node recipe retains inventory and evidence
but removes incoming edges, with counts corrected. Duplicate-node counts
describe the distinct-node view so count mismatch cannot mask uniqueness.
Additional fixed probes protect root digest and version, catalog byte pin,
review/declaration agreement, independently supplied assessment scope,
whole-version review scope, unresolved diagnostics and graph node/edge
uniqueness. The positive alternate-filename probe rejects reinstatement of
the removed `LICENSE.txt` restriction.

The three owner probes individually change license sentinel, basis or
disposition while preserving all other bindings and the other dependency's
complete incompatible evidence. Their ordinary result is unresolved; weakening
the individual prerequisite incorrectly yields `owner-evidence-required`.
The missing-policy/catalog weakening deliberately supplies the known pinned
test buffers as an ambient default. The inventory weakening deliberately
collapses duplicates, ignores extras and evaluates only the inventory/graph
intersection. These are explicit **bad implementations**, not authorized
normal fallback behavior. The missing-assessment weakening deliberately
invents scope from the receipt; the ordinary classifier never does so.

The required guard-ID list is independently enumerated, manual and finite.
Every registered ID must have a nonempty list of known, discriminating witness
IDs; unknown/duplicate IDs, orphan witnesses and equal normal/wrong branches
fail. The meta-check removes each of the 66 registrations and separately
empties each witness list: **132/132 omissions must reject**. Removing a
registration therefore cannot silently shrink the required inventory.

The normal and optimized commands run the entire fixed suite. There is no
source rewriting, dynamic evaluation, arbitrary mutation generation/search,
package execution, model evaluation or production mutation. The inventory
records normative guard families, not a second language/runtime grammar or
an exhaustive proof against every possible incorrect implementation.

### Explicit finite guard table

Witness IDs below are the exact IDs in `discrimination.json`; that document
also freezes each rule's description, source case, normal branch, wrong
branch and, when applicable, fixed recipe and weakened rule.
`replay:` uses an unchanged existing packet with a weakened rule;
`memory:` uses one fixed in-memory recipe; `branch:` rechecks an unchanged
case's branch without claiming a weakened-oracle execution.

